      "documentConcept": "Acupuncture, massage and traditional adjuncts in PD",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:d3764b4cf6db019698d9a6362f391e8e30d9c39ecccbe00aea1a8407a6be5239",
      "text": "Evidence read for acupuncture, massage, the Alexander Technique and other traditional adjuncts in Parkinson's disease (PD). Two older meta-analyses found no significant UPDRS advantage of real over placebo acupuncture; later syntheses of acupuncture combined with conventional treatment report UPDRS gains but describe the evidence as limited, and an overview rated all included reviews very-low-quality. Sham-controlled trials show a sleep signal and no fatigue benefit over sham. No serious adverse events were reported. Massage meta-analyses report motor benefit alongside methodological concerns. NICE NG71 says to consider the Alexander Technique for balance or motor function problems; a complementary-medicine guideline grades acupuncture added to drugs 'should be considered' on low-level evidence; Parkinson's UK does not endorse any particular therapies.",
      "sources": [
        "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11208974/",
